        "math_academy_id": "q-98211",
        "problem": "Calculate \n${\\int }_{2}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$\ngiven that\n${\\int }_{2}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\u27e85,\\,-1,\\,2\u27e9$\nand\n${\\int }_{3}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\u27e8-2,\\,-4,\\,3\u27e9.$",
        "worked_solution": "Using the adjacent intervals rule, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{2}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{3}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t={\\int }_{2}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{3}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nFinally, using the given information, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{3}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{3}^{4}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\u27e85,\\,-1,\\,2\u27e9-\u27e8-2,\\,-4,\\,3\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e87,\\,3,\\,-1\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "actual_result": "Correct",
